      "rationale": "The archived evidence showed the x^2 term gave almost no NMSE gain (0.09694 -> 0.09639), so curvature in x is not the missing structure. The persistent diagnostic signature is instead amplitude-dependent: rmse in the highest-v bin (0.0322) is roughly 1.7-2.5x that of the other v bins (0.012-0.019), and that bin's mean residual is the largest positive v-bin mean (0.0017). Those bins are association only, not proof of a mechanism, so I frame this as a hypothesis test: replacing the x^2 term with a velocity-amplitude damping term keeps the physically motivated linear oscillator and tests whether the error grows with |v| as amplitude-dependent damping would predict. I declare n_params=3 and set the linear signs explicitly so the bounded least-squares fit is not dependent on absorbing the physical minus sign into the constants. Prediction: NMSE should decrease (high-|v| bins currently dominate total error) by a small absolute amount, at least 0.002.",
